    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Only `AcceptanceTest` is a runnable test class, and it passes along with a successful build (which compiles the generated DTO, the mapper, and the new helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` field on the owner read model, satisfying the spec: **true** when the owner is a possible duplicate, its email domain is disposable-adjacent, or its city is over its soft capacity; otherwise **false**.\n\nFollowing the codebase's established idiom (small cohesive model helpers like `PossibleDuplicate`, `OwnerSegment`, `MembershipLevel` wired in via MapStruct expressions), I kept structural impact low with three minimal touches:\n\n1. **New `model/RiskFlag.java`** \u2014 a small, self-contained helper. `of(owner)` ORs the three signals; a private `disposableAdjacent(email)` treats a domain as disposable-adjacent when it equals a known disposable provider or is a subdomain of one (the exact domains are already rejected at create time by `NonDisposableEmailValidator`, so this \"adjacent\" reading catches the subdomain cases that slip past). The two stored signals reuse the existing `possibleDuplicate` and `capacityWarning` fields captured at creation.\n\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one added `@Mapping` line delegating to `RiskFlag.of(owner)`, mirroring every other derived field. The changed method stays CC 1 with little surrounding context.\n\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` read-only boolean to the `OwnerDto` schema so the generated DTO carries the field.\n\nNo existing method grew and no large class was modified; behaviour was added as an isolated unit. The `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` acceptance test (unique owner, no email \u2192 all three signals false) returns `false` as required.",
